#
# prima-materia — knowing the text as the condition, and not knowing it as the failure
#
# One correspondence, formed on 2026-09-19 out of two readings carried in the
# same run: the Vedic Brāhmaṇas and the Egyptian Book of the Dead. It is held
# here rather than in either tradition file because it belongs to neither, and
# it is entered at all because the two witnesses lie on opposite sides of the
# contact question this collection is short of.
#

pmc:KnowingTheTextAsTheCondition a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "The attainment is attached to knowing a text, and the failure to not knowing it"@en, "Die Erlangung hängt am Wissen eines Textes, und das Scheitern am Nichtwissen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two transmissions state the condition of their attainments in the same unusual place. Neither says that the man who performs the rite obtains the result; both say that the man who knows obtains it. The Egyptian corpus closes chapter after chapter with the clause that if this chapter be known by the deceased, such and such will follow — coming forth by day, passage at every gate, transformation into whatever form he pleases, flourishing children and the favour of the king. The Vedic corpus closes exposition after exposition with the clause that he who knows this attains a full life, offspring, cattle, prosperity, or escape from the death that comes again. And both state the reverse in the same terms. At the hall of the two Maati every part of the doorway refuses the dead man passage unless he tells its name; in the Vedic dialogue on the fires that go out, the eldest son dies, the master of the house dies, the cattle die, but only in the case of him who would not know this, while the man who knows has by dint of knowledge prevailed."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:AegyptiaRubricaScientiae, pmc:AegyptiaPortaeNomina, pmc:VaidikaYaEvamVeda, pmc:VaidikaJnanenaJitam ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:EgyptianRitual ;
    pm:independenceGround "Not a claim of priority in time. The Egyptian witness is the older by a wide margin: the Book of the Dead is attested from about sixteen hundred before this era and its clause-bearing predecessors earlier still, while the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa in the Mādhyandina recension belongs to the first half of the following millennium. What carries the independence is that no route of contact has been established, in anything this run read or searched, between the Egyptian mortuary literature and the Vedic ritual exegesis for this feature; the disputed contact route entered on the Egyptian instance concerns the Graeco-Roman and hermetic material of a much later period and the demotic manuscripts of the Roman era, not the Indian side, and the documented route entered on the Vedic instance concerns its own Indic and Eurasian neighbourhood. Two things this ground does not settle and does not hide. Both corpora stand in one connected Old World in which contact of other kinds is abundantly documented, and absence of an established route for a feature is not the same as an established absence of route. And the feature is of a kind for which the structural alternative is strong: a transmission whose product is a text has a reason of its own to make knowing the text the condition, and that reason is available to anybody independently of anybody else. See the note below and generalizations/the-text-and-what-is-withheld.ttl, where the structural reading is what is actually put at risk." ;
    skos:note "Why the correspondence is worth stating at all, given that caveat. It is not that two transmissions promise things for knowing — many do. It is that both of them state the failure in the same place and in the same grammar, so that the whole apparatus turns on one unobservable fact about a man. In the Egyptian case the gates ask a question whose answer the buyer has already purchased; in the Vedic case the same mishap kills one man's son and not another's, and what decides it is an equivalence held in the mind. In both, no third party at any time could tell the two cases apart."@en ;
    skos:note "What the two do not share, and it is worth as much as what they do. The Egyptian condition is knowledge of a particular wording that can be written on a roll and buried with the man, and the corpus is that roll. The Vedic condition is knowledge of an identification — that this breath has entered that breath, that the year is Death — which the corpus states but which is not a formula to be recited. One is a password, the other an interpretation. A run that wanted to press the correspondence further would have to ask whether the Egyptian names are ever glossed as meanings rather than as names."@en ;
    skos:note "A third witness stands near and is deliberately not counted. The Hekhalot literature in this graph has doorkeepers at the palaces, a stated fitness, and a penalty of violence for the descender who fails at the sixth gate; but what that gate tests is silence and not knowledge of a name, and the passages where names and seals are the condition of passage were not read at the wording in this run. It is named here so that the next run knows where the third leg would come from, and it is not in pm:convergingConcept."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-material was looked for in this holding before the node was written, and one kind was found. Where a transmission deliberately keeps part of its operative content out of writing, the condition moves off the text: the internal alchemy says that the sages transmit the medicine and do not transmit the fire, and that the one aperture cannot be settled by one's own notion but must be handed on from mouth to mind. That does not contradict this correspondence, which is about two corpora that put everything into the text; it bounds it, and the bound is the useful part. It is stated as a claim at generalizations/the-text-and-what-is-withheld.ttl."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapters 64, 83, 86 and 125, rubrics and the address to the gods of the hall of the two Maati, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, The Book of the Dead: An English Translation of the Chapters, Hymns, etc., of the Theban Recension, London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1901, vol. I, pp. 217-218 and 221-222, and vol. II, pp. 269, 275-277 and 375-378", "Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa XI,5,3,8-12 and XI,5,6,9 (Mādhyandina), in the English of Julius Eggeling, The Śatapatha-Brāhmaṇa, Sacred Books of the East 44, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1900, pp. 82-85 and 99", "Aitareya Brāhmaṇa iii,7 to iii,11 (xi,7 to xi,11), in the English of Arthur Berriedale Keith, Rigveda Brahmanas, Harvard Oriental Series 25, Cambridge, Massachusetts 1920, pp. 169-172" .
